+/*
+ * Wait for at least `us` microseconds, but never wait forever.
+ *
+ * The physical counter is the nice way to do this, and on the SoCs this loader
+ * was first written for it is always running: the boot ROM has set up the
+ * timebase long before us.  It is not something we can assume on every board
+ * though.  What happens when CNTPCT_EL0 does not advance is not a short delay,
+ * it is a hang: `while (ticks < end)` never becomes false, so the whole boot
+ * stops dead at the first delay on the path.  That is exactly how a board with
+ * a stopped counter looks from outside -- a screen showing whatever was drawn
+ * before the first dbg_delay_ms() and nothing after it, for as long as you are
+ * willing to watch.
+ *
+ * So every wait here is bounded twice: by the counter, and by a fixed amount of
+ * spinning.  If the counter is stuck at one value it will take the spin budget
+ * and carry on.  The budget is calibrated so that a running counter always wins
+ * -- on a real timebase the loop exits on the first condition long before the
+ * budget is reached -- which means the spin path only ever costs us anything on
